#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542613 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d630ca6-fd28-58ed-995a-8da968fd5ba9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8 \\sqrt{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542560} {:answer/id #uuid "4c0613bc-32a4-53a2-a943-9e50b992e369" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16 \\sqrt{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542567} {:answer/id #uuid "5fa57f44-2642-593c-a3e7-ef6c93fe59eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 16 \\sqrt{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542570} {:answer/id #uuid "867db4e2-70dd-5f07-8dc4-522fdb1b0abe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542578} {:answer/id #uuid "b4af4815-a8ff-5192-b441-27ae750e4043" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "160" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542587}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4c0613bc-32a4-53a2-a943-9e50b992e369" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16 \\sqrt{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542567} :answer-field/id #uuid "7d7d77a5-ca55-5835-beb8-b8509e3fef56" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542601}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fa2eae58-23f0-53d8-954b-616c559e82a9" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)=2 x$ along the line $y=3 x,$ $\\,0\\le x\\le 4.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $y=y(x).$ Therefore, by setting $x=t,$ we have $y=y(t)=3 t.$ This gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle t,3 t\\rangle,\\qquad 0\\le t\\le 4\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)$ and $\\|\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)\\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)&=\\frac{\\mathrm dx}{\\mathrm dt}\\mathbf i+\\frac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dt}\\mathbf j \\\\ &=\\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}(t)\\mathbf i+\\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}(3 t)\\mathbf j \\\\ &=\\mathbf i+(3)\\mathbf j \\\\ \\|\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm dx}{\\mathrm dt}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dt}\\right)^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{1^2+(3)^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=2 x$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t))&=2 t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm ds&=\\int_{0}^{4}f(r(t))\\|\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{4}(2 t)(\\sqrt{10})\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ &=\\left[\\sqrt{10} t^{2}\\right]_{0}^{4} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{10}\\left((4)^{2}-(0)^{2}\\right) \\\\ &=\\sqrt{10}\\left(16-0\\right) \\\\ &=16 \\sqrt{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543680 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22fabb63-36cb-5c3f-98b6-b4f2b8a1b771" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{\\pi}\\int_{0}^{2}r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543688} {:answer/id #uuid "290abe19-a5e8-5f16-ac8a-3c254b59ca02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{\\pi}\\int_{0}^{4 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}1\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543689} {:answer/id #uuid "a87eed07-0b8a-5520-9c6e-109e5f040429" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{\\pi}\\int_{0}^{- 4 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543712} {:answer/id #uuid "b5fcfdca-6265-5fab-9691-6ac1a660390c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{0}^{4 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543717} {:answer/id #uuid "ecf036c2-6040-5864-81c8-69e069735579" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{\\pi}\\int_{0}^{4 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543724}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ecf036c2-6040-5864-81c8-69e069735579" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{\\pi}\\int_{0}^{4 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543724} :answer-field/id #uuid "04b50b83-c4ee-50ea-ae8a-d5cc1f4efc8b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543679}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "06448e69-2e5e-5172-aee5-b3151a22cc6e" :question/problem "If $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x^{2}+(y-2)^{2}\\le 4\\},$ then $$\n\\displaystyle \\iint_D 1\\,\\mathrm{d}A=\n$$\n\nChoose the polar setup whose bounds describe $D$ with $r\\ge 0$ and whose integrand is the given Cartesian integrand multiplied by the Jacobian $r.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iint_D f(x,y)\\,dA=\\iint_\\Delta f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch our region $D:$\n\n![](images/4f/4ff66220bf03e9c5dde372e20aa060fa26a4a57ab481a61845726fde8ca25046.png)\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents the disk of radius $2$ centered at $(0,2).$\n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\cos\\theta \\\\ y=r\\sin\\theta\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the $x^{2}+(y-2)^{2}=4$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x^{2}+(y-2)^{2}&=4\\\\(r\\cos\\theta)^2+(r\\sin\\theta-2)^2&=4\\\\r^2\\cos^2\\theta+r^2\\sin^2\\theta-4r\\sin\\theta+4&=4\\\\r^2(\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta)-4r\\sin\\theta&=0\\\\r^2-4r\\sin\\theta&=0\\\\r(r-4\\sin\\theta)&=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire circle, we must have $r=4 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}.$\n\nNow, let's determine the limits for $r$ and $\\theta.$ First of all, $r$ can vary from $r=0$ (at the origin) to $r=4 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}$ (at the boundary of the region):\n\n![](images/88/882f7487d40a724c331aebb3dd954b8beace88ff5a2e65b4b2b9abedd74885dc.png)\n\nSecondly, $\\theta$ can vary between $\\theta=0$ and $\\theta=\\pi.$\n\n![](images/41/410279d76b3d76135133c63fe19003839b783a5d4177aff6744bd950fcbe93bd.png)\n\nSo, in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta),$ the region can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 4 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)},\\,0\\le\\theta\\le \\pi\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D 1\\,dA&=\\iint_\\Delta (1)\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta\\\\&=\\int_{0}^{\\pi}\\int_{0}^{4 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543704 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0afc24b7-4e9b-5fc1-a813-41887ebcebbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543681} {:answer/id #uuid "0ffe947f-52e1-584d-be76-77ece40d3f41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{0}^{4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543682} {:answer/id #uuid "196f500a-a2af-50e3-9dd1-0ecadbc6d54a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{2}r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543685} {:answer/id #uuid "2273c691-6884-5960-9489-5d738197d00e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}1\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543687} {:answer/id #uuid "e21ac4be-6b11-5399-80cc-05afed3bd495" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{- 4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543722}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0afc24b7-4e9b-5fc1-a813-41887ebcebbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543681} :answer-field/id #uuid "c9146589-ac3d-5d74-94be-b61e9289276d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543719}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7d4f7134-3d22-5cbc-8e83-54b913ada494" :question/problem "If $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,(x-2)^{2}+y^{2}\\le 4\\},$ then $$\n\\displaystyle \\iint_D 1\\,\\mathrm{d}A=\n$$\n\nChoose the polar setup whose bounds describe $D$ with $r\\ge 0$ and whose integrand is the given Cartesian integrand multiplied by the Jacobian $r.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iint_D f(x,y)\\,dA=\\iint_\\Delta f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch our region $D:$\n\n![](images/86/866f3162cd881e997f28cea68fe22fc90b8ef011b6d8beff00ef38d9a69d2eb9.png)\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents the disk of radius $2$ centered at $(2,0).$\n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\cos\\theta \\\\ y=r\\sin\\theta\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the $(x-2)^{2}+y^{2}=4$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2}+y^{2}&=4\\\\(r\\cos\\theta-2)^2+(r\\sin\\theta)^2&=4\\\\r^2\\cos^2\\theta-4r\\cos\\theta+4+r^2\\sin^2\\theta&=4\\\\r^2(\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta)-4r\\cos\\theta&=0\\\\r^2-4r\\cos\\theta&=0\\\\r(r-4\\cos\\theta)&=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire circle, we must have $r=4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}.$\n\nNow, let's determine the limits for $r$ and $\\theta.$ First of all, $r$ can vary from $r=0$ (at the origin) to $r=4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}$ (at the boundary of the region):\n\n![](images/6c/6c841a1dc73b8a68ba8d51bb4d0f8d1ff051f8d1c0bafdfdbfac134ccbc651aa.png)\n\nSecondly, $\\theta$ can vary between $\\theta=- \\frac{\\pi}{2}$ and $\\theta=\\frac{\\pi}{2}.$\n\n![](images/08/083360143d05d210dcbd11ed86c3a5cc0f2510beeecb68a210e9f52632609867.png)\n\nSo, in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta),$ the region can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)},\\,- \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\le\\theta\\le \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D 1\\,dA&=\\iint_\\Delta (1)\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta\\\\&=\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543707 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15cbb851-3dfc-5f92-8728-a8b8eec6ed2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543684} {:answer/id #uuid "21ffbbe1-7ca1-5c19-9aef-3f70c88274e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{- 4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543686} {:answer/id #uuid "298cf0ac-ab43-5882-91a6-e7348499b76f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{2}r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543690} {:answer/id #uuid "3e7adafb-1edd-5777-af5d-4c1cdf3fb5bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{0}^{- 4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543695} {:answer/id #uuid "ad2bb1be-7c1c-50a0-8af1-048b30c61a71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{- 4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}1\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543715}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "21ffbbe1-7ca1-5c19-9aef-3f70c88274e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{- 4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543686} :answer-field/id #uuid "c09a0523-101d-5678-835e-b363f24d33b3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543718}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9021eafc-a91a-5e03-85b9-0baff1b49b29" :question/problem "If $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,(x+2)^{2}+y^{2}\\le 4\\},$ then $$\n\\displaystyle \\iint_D 1\\,\\mathrm{d}A=\n$$\n\nChoose the polar setup whose bounds describe $D$ with $r\\ge 0$ and whose integrand is the given Cartesian integrand multiplied by the Jacobian $r.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iint_D f(x,y)\\,dA=\\iint_\\Delta f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch our region $D:$\n\n![](images/3a/3a77e854cc9553675752e3e4e231c8915d390091a4f4fa22cee2eb5ffe279c3e.png)\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents the disk of radius $2$ centered at $(-2,0).$\n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\cos\\theta \\\\ y=r\\sin\\theta\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the $(x+2)^{2}+y^{2}=4$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+2)^{2}+y^{2}&=4\\\\(r\\cos\\theta+2)^2+(r\\sin\\theta)^2&=4\\\\r^2\\cos^2\\theta+4r\\cos\\theta+4+r^2\\sin^2\\theta&=4\\\\r^2(\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta)+4r\\cos\\theta&=0\\\\r^2+4r\\cos\\theta&=0\\\\r(r+4\\cos\\theta)&=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire circle, we must have $r=- 4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}.$\n\nNow, let's determine the limits for $r$ and $\\theta.$ First of all, $r$ can vary from $r=0$ (at the origin) to $r=- 4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}$ (at the boundary of the region):\n\n![](images/a9/a934a520dfc672601e31dd73fc71a06505f41b6088325a30b49f4d7d99d0f0d7.png)\n\nSecondly, $\\theta$ can vary between $\\theta=\\frac{\\pi}{2}$ and $\\theta=\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}.$\n\n![](images/d5/d5b43bd12ba3ca3b3dd69560c728f9c7b16d2d8331702cc3921e8457f53c294f.png)\n\nSo, in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta),$ the region can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le - 4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)},\\,\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\le\\theta\\le \\frac{3 \\pi}{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D 1\\,dA&=\\iint_\\Delta (1)\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta\\\\&=\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{- 4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543716 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3543a771-464b-542c-9335-538c3709149f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{6 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}1\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543691} {:answer/id #uuid "4528835f-33a6-5ec5-9a47-a775cb974623" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{- 6 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543696} {:answer/id #uuid "487a6fa0-57bb-500c-9371-92da4be661ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{6 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543697} {:answer/id #uuid "86580f35-f153-55b3-b4bc-2e64e0c0b121" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{3}r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543706} {:answer/id #uuid "ac2b1f00-489c-598a-af58-abeb3809e74d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{0}^{6 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543714}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "487a6fa0-57bb-500c-9371-92da4be661ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{6 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543697} :answer-field/id #uuid "980036b8-3088-5457-9263-9a3e9604d20c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543709}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b2b48fc3-a0dd-5bed-af7d-8c8aed9c00ae" :question/problem "If $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,(x-3)^{2}+y^{2}\\le 9\\},$ then $$\n\\displaystyle \\iint_D 1\\,\\mathrm{d}A=\n$$\n\nChoose the polar setup whose bounds describe $D$ with $r\\ge 0$ and whose integrand is the given Cartesian integrand multiplied by the Jacobian $r.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iint_D f(x,y)\\,dA=\\iint_\\Delta f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch our region $D:$\n\n![](images/74/74735ff47608d314c82b66367404f6d2bb79c0c1e34b034083ebcd2d74dc55c0.png)\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents the disk of radius $3$ centered at $(3,0).$\n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\cos\\theta \\\\ y=r\\sin\\theta\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the $(x-3)^{2}+y^{2}=9$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-3)^{2}+y^{2}&=9\\\\(r\\cos\\theta-3)^2+(r\\sin\\theta)^2&=9\\\\r^2\\cos^2\\theta-6r\\cos\\theta+9+r^2\\sin^2\\theta&=9\\\\r^2(\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta)-6r\\cos\\theta&=0\\\\r^2-6r\\cos\\theta&=0\\\\r(r-6\\cos\\theta)&=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire circle, we must have $r=6 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}.$\n\nNow, let's determine the limits for $r$ and $\\theta.$ First of all, $r$ can vary from $r=0$ (at the origin) to $r=6 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}$ (at the boundary of the region):\n\n![](images/8b/8bafd3851f0378006c6e851b9bb60ab4bbcb1dadafffe4d5b5082d304ca7fed7.png)\n\nSecondly, $\\theta$ can vary between $\\theta=- \\frac{\\pi}{2}$ and $\\theta=\\frac{\\pi}{2}.$\n\n![](images/cc/ccdbd4ea144224d5793d4c48018147ca939cba611e5ea38ac649295218ca2f07.png)\n\nSo, in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta),$ the region can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 6 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)},\\,- \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\le\\theta\\le \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D 1\\,dA&=\\iint_\\Delta (1)\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta\\\\&=\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{6 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543720 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4acdd0df-c785-59f2-9658-4e3a960a5cfe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2π}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{3\\cos θ}r^{3}drdθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543698} {:answer/id #uuid "5031c2db-393e-5fdd-8e60-3997b7fff3b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{- π/2}^{π/2}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{3\\cos θ}r^{2}\\cos 2θdrdθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543701} {:answer/id #uuid "96c17cb2-2129-5e02-9bdf-4663faa9470d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{- π/2}^{π/2}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{\\frac{3}{2}}r^{3}drdθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543708} {:answer/id #uuid "a19c980b-b72c-5250-b5c3-5c4d8d144b94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{- π/2}^{π/2}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{3\\cos θ}r^{3}\\cos 2θdrdθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543711} {:answer/id #uuid "e5698084-b769-5ac6-b65b-adb1e670787d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2π}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{\\frac{3}{2}}r^{3}\\cos 2θdrdθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543723}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a19c980b-b72c-5250-b5c3-5c4d8d144b94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{- π/2}^{π/2}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{3\\cos θ}r^{3}\\cos 2θdrdθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543711} :answer-field/id #uuid "aafcfd12-6869-527e-91e6-ff53db154e0f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543713}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d3a13c3d-8749-55b1-8f45-ee637a70eea2" :question/problem "If $$\n\\displaystyle D = \\{(x, y): (x - \\frac{3}{2})^{2} + y^{2} \\le \\frac{9}{4}\\},\n$$ then $$\n\\displaystyle ∬_(D)(x^{2} - y^{2})dA =\n$$\n\nChoose the polar setup whose bounds describe $D$ with $r\\ge 0$ and whose integrand is the given Cartesian integrand multiplied by the Jacobian $r.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iint_D f(x,y)\\,dA=\\iint_\\Delta f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch our region $D:$\n\n![](images/d0/d01daaf1f81b8dd2b2ab8d01bbbe5a7dd1f4262eb7de9d3977b8c1e104a90ec0.png)\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents the disk of radius $\\frac{3}{2}$ centered at $(\\frac{3}{2},0).$\n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\cos\\theta \\\\ y=r\\sin\\theta\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the $(x-\\frac{3}{2})^{2}+y^{2}=\\frac{9}{4}$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-\\frac{3}{2})^{2}+y^{2}&=\\frac{9}{4}\\\\(r\\cos\\theta-\\frac{3}{2})^2+(r\\sin\\theta)^2&=\\frac{9}{4}\\\\r^2\\cos^2\\theta-3r\\cos\\theta+\\frac{9}{4}+r^2\\sin^2\\theta&=\\frac{9}{4}\\\\r^2(\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta)-3r\\cos\\theta&=0\\\\r^2-3r\\cos\\theta&=0\\\\r(r-3\\cos\\theta)&=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire circle, we must have $r=3 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}.$\n\nNow, let's determine the limits for $r$ and $\\theta.$ First of all, $r$ can vary from $r=0$ (at the origin) to $r=3 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}$ (at the boundary of the region):\n\n![](images/ac/ac0374d74edf7f4490f7c160b929b9dc5d03d1324d7cb79dc691a5ca21248e35.png)\n\nSecondly, $\\theta$ can vary between $\\theta=- \\frac{\\pi}{2}$ and $\\theta=\\frac{\\pi}{2}.$\n\n![](images/c6/c674039d133057398ff5fd98770479e9fba7866524ead09c78adba2c2ac5cb80.png)\n\nSo, in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta),$ the region can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 3 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)},\\,- \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\le\\theta\\le \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D x^{2} - y^{2}\\,dA&=\\iint_\\Delta (- r^{2} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + r^{2} \\cos^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)})\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta\\\\&=\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{3 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r^3(\\cos^2\\theta-\\sin^2\\theta)\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\\\\&=\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{3 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r^{3} \\cos{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543721 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "010f0bb3-cfce-57dc-a3dd-0e6bf16e313e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543678} {:answer/id #uuid "4cde6b07-0e2a-568b-abd2-30c5e1ac404b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543699} {:answer/id #uuid "63130042-5901-5034-86fc-435727d0f3f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543702} {:answer/id #uuid "7ee5d5f6-e473-541e-87b4-e95658ad2cff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543705} {:answer/id #uuid "f0fe3285-6e13-5d53-953e-91a811e1db98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543725}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4cde6b07-0e2a-568b-abd2-30c5e1ac404b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543699} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d84f478-b5b5-5f25-9156-e2d90cf503dc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543693}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dc21b297-d346-5eac-82b2-8f472263113f" :question/problem "If $$\n\\displaystyle D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+(y-\\frac{3}{2})^{2}\\le \\frac{9}{4}\\},\n$$ then $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}x\\,\\text{d}A=\n$$\n\nChoose the polar setup whose bounds describe $D$ with $r\\ge 0$ and whose integrand is the given Cartesian integrand multiplied by the Jacobian $r.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA={∬}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch our region $D:$\n\n![](images/07/074eb42aff2c78d93136fc9e56b2420450f72c5d34c6b12da8bf84e080db75ea.png)\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents the disk of radius $\\frac{3}{2}$ centered at $(0,\\frac{3}{2}).$\n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the ${x}^{2}+(y-\\frac{3}{2})^{2}=\\frac{9}{4}$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y-\\frac{3}{2})^{2} & =\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ {(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} & =\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -3r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{9}{4} & =\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-3r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-3r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire circle, we must have $r=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$\n\nNow, let's determine the limits for $r$ and $\\theta .$ First of all, $r$ can vary from $r=0$ (at the origin) to $r=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ (at the boundary of the region): \n\n![](images/ce/cea2b2ddd6b211073b579d71bd8ed08405fa04e354825a124acb71bedf413295.png)\n\nSecondly, $\\theta $ can vary between $\\theta =0$ (the positive $x$-axis) and $\\theta =\\pi $ (the negative $x$-axis):\n\n![](images/bb/bbe07b71b3c82cde2e9175b06904905206984b04ca9a8ccd746b051212f5e8d8.png)\n\nSo, in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}x\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543726 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "154fe4ca-a591-51af-b5a1-03adc5261ae6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{\\pi}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{0}^{4 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543683} {:answer/id #uuid "3cc7ec97-c47c-54d7-b497-f40e8148a957" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{\\pi}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{0}^{- 4 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543692} {:answer/id #uuid "3e0c7a54-ad42-5b46-9438-a5e86783d08d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{\\pi}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{0}^{2}r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543694} {:answer/id #uuid "4d85821c-4168-5a6a-994e-4c67538a143d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{0}^{- 4 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543700} {:answer/id #uuid "9f993769-4242-5c50-a578-e44a67fa0267" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{\\pi}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{0}^{- 4 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}1\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543710}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3cc7ec97-c47c-54d7-b497-f40e8148a957" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{\\pi}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{0}^{- 4 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543692} :answer-field/id #uuid "6397bb0a-9d78-551e-a3e8-a73e2230a72a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543703}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fa53fde6-b454-5423-91b4-30fdb6c5d5b2" :question/problem "If $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x^{2}+(y+2)^{2}\\le 4\\},$ then $$\n\\displaystyle \\iint_D 1\\,\\mathrm{d}A=\n$$\n\nChoose the polar setup whose bounds describe $D$ with $r\\ge 0$ and whose integrand is the given Cartesian integrand multiplied by the Jacobian $r.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iint_D f(x,y)\\,dA=\\iint_\\Delta f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch our region $D:$\n\n![](images/57/57ffd44d5406aafa448571f9a56eebded9a9916def605c08eb4eb1ad1e0df56c.png)\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents the disk of radius $2$ centered at $(0,-2).$\n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\cos\\theta \\\\ y=r\\sin\\theta\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the $x^{2}+(y+2)^{2}=4$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x^{2}+(y+2)^{2}&=4\\\\(r\\cos\\theta)^2+(r\\sin\\theta+2)^2&=4\\\\r^2\\cos^2\\theta+r^2\\sin^2\\theta+4r\\sin\\theta+4&=4\\\\r^2(\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta)+4r\\sin\\theta&=0\\\\r^2+4r\\sin\\theta&=0\\\\r(r+4\\sin\\theta)&=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire circle, we must have $r=- 4 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}.$\n\nNow, let's determine the limits for $r$ and $\\theta.$ First of all, $r$ can vary from $r=0$ (at the origin) to $r=- 4 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}$ (at the boundary of the region):\n\n![](images/0b/0b3138e7b6599e8174ccd46648b334b18a505757b845e53af59d0b98e3b4c92b.png)\n\nSecondly, $\\theta$ can vary between $\\theta=\\pi$ and $\\theta=2 \\pi.$\n\n![](images/11/11dcaf5545227d8231eef23ee29ce52723be103c8a209a578927cacb83d56384.png)\n\nSo, in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta),$ the region can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le - 4 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)},\\,\\pi\\le\\theta\\le 2 \\pi\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D 1\\,dA&=\\iint_\\Delta (1)\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta\\\\&=\\int_{\\pi}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{0}^{- 4 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543812 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "576b4a58-c183-5275-bda2-05fe1ba9c5fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{59}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543815} {:answer/id #uuid "7ff2ff02-02b1-5751-b12a-7e9ef64a27e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{97}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543820} {:answer/id #uuid "857a4020-99d9-5010-bea5-959744696366" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{59}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543821} {:answer/id #uuid "a3810977-df2d-5104-b3e0-f2a4d0401184" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543827} {:answer/id #uuid "f91c3520-3e82-5867-91f7-0be0499e3c49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543838}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "857a4020-99d9-5010-bea5-959744696366" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{59}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543821} :answer-field/id #uuid "4ab8a066-f63f-5bc4-a451-8cc061d5437d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543813}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2a13fd10-2736-5163-8e91-3a139268a2d1" :question/problem "![](images/5e/5e11606627f4b91c977670cf9f919aff8b659cb5b4776cf3d04bb561b50b79fb.png)\n\nSuppose that $F$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ defined over the region $D$ above. Given that \n\n$$\n{∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=- \\frac{17}{2},\\,{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=- \\frac{19}{3},\\,{∮}_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=\\frac{23}{3},\n$$\n\nwhere ${C}_{1},$ ${C}_{2},$ and ${C}_{3}$ are oriented clockwise, calculate the value of $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the total circulation of a vector field over the boundary of a region containing holes, we add together the circulations over each *positively oriented* boundary.\n\nRemember that:\n\n- the positive direction is *counterclockwise* for the *outer* curve ${C}_{1}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{1}$ counterclockwise), and\n\n- the positive direction is *clockwise* for the *inner* curves ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ clockwise).\n\nThe diagram below shows the positive orientations of all three curves.\n\n![](images/fa/fa1022d2a1718627898289abdd4d3035a6e06230eea16d0e46832675b2e37def.png)\n\nNotice that the positive orientation of the outer curve ${C}_{1}$ is opposite that of ${C}_{1}$ as stated in the question.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & =-{∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{∮}_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =-\\left(- \\frac{17}{2}\\right)+\\left(- \\frac{19}{3}\\right)+\\frac{23}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{-(-51)+(-38)+46}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{59}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543825 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02fb5c25-2a44-5658-8d3a-94fee2cfc5f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543806} {:answer/id #uuid "08456781-dc15-5446-8a0d-a30462b07db6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{37}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543809} {:answer/id #uuid "5aa28b88-1b4c-5f38-a927-2f0a3b55f78c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543817} {:answer/id #uuid "73ed4b27-596d-5370-95af-6d3edadf7860" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{20}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543818} {:answer/id #uuid "950d0158-2f90-5533-a993-2f44cea84fca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{58}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543822}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5aa28b88-1b4c-5f38-a927-2f0a3b55f78c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543817} :answer-field/id #uuid "10013332-1268-5bf0-abb7-b697ec1223bc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543811}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9be43ff2-1cbd-5518-ad47-108fe13ce12b" :question/problem "![](images/5e/5e11606627f4b91c977670cf9f919aff8b659cb5b4776cf3d04bb561b50b79fb.png)\n\nSuppose that $F$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ defined over the region $D$ above. Given that \n\n$$\n{∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=\\frac{19}{3},\\,{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=- \\frac{7}{2},\\,{∮}_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=\\frac{19}{2},\n$$\n\nwhere ${C}_{1},$ ${C}_{2},$ and ${C}_{3}$ are oriented clockwise, calculate the value of $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the total circulation of a vector field over the boundary of a region containing holes, we add together the circulations over each *positively oriented* boundary.\n\nRemember that:\n\n- the positive direction is *counterclockwise* for the *outer* curve ${C}_{1}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{1}$ counterclockwise), and\n\n- the positive direction is *clockwise* for the *inner* curves ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ clockwise).\n\nThe diagram below shows the positive orientations of all three curves.\n\n![](images/fa/fa1022d2a1718627898289abdd4d3035a6e06230eea16d0e46832675b2e37def.png)\n\nNotice that the positive orientation of the outer curve ${C}_{1}$ is opposite that of ${C}_{1}$ as stated in the question.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & =-{∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{∮}_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =-\\frac{19}{3}+\\left(- \\frac{7}{2}\\right)+\\frac{19}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-38+(-21)+57}{6} \\\\  & =- \\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543832 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "545c9291-08a2-5911-a792-6f15761476b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{121}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543814} {:answer/id #uuid "7a00a83b-01e3-57a2-a8d3-f075c520f92d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{121}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543819} {:answer/id #uuid "a7c85235-d1f2-5afc-ad26-668d508f8d3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{41}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543828} {:answer/id #uuid "f84a1e97-fe94-5bb5-9e84-50b64ebd8913" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{89}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543837} {:answer/id #uuid "fb233b92-516e-521b-865e-7132bfd10fc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{71}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543840}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7a00a83b-01e3-57a2-a8d3-f075c520f92d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{121}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543819} :answer-field/id #uuid "07139094-3747-5fd0-adf6-264c86178a78" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543808}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ca868da8-dd46-5274-aa8f-98c77655ddad" :question/problem "![](images/cb/cbe865077b446bdd97c290cef0924d3f17807a9e1039c9be45f4aa6e4b29cc3c.png)\n\nSuppose that $F$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ defined over the region $D$ above. Given that \n\n$$\n{∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=- \\frac{25}{3},\\,{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=\\frac{16}{3},\\,{∮}_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=\\frac{13}{2},\n$$\n\nwhere ${C}_{1},$ ${C}_{2},$ and ${C}_{3}$ are oriented counterclockwise, calculate the value of $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the total circulation of a vector field over the boundary of a region containing holes, we add together the circulations over each *positively oriented* boundary.\n\nRemember that:\n\n- the positive direction is *counterclockwise* for the *outer* curve ${C}_{1}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{1}$ counterclockwise), and\n\n- the positive direction is *clockwise* for the *inner* curves ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ clockwise).\n\nThe diagram below shows the positive orientations of all three curves.\n\n![](images/fd/fd221ae68fc4983fb0a3f7a745154764e5c0081b2b9b66e7f8ad4a150a8b3bc5.png)\n\nNotice that the positive orientations of the inner curves ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ are opposite to those stated in the question.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{∮}_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =\\left(- \\frac{25}{3}\\right)-\\frac{16}{3}-\\frac{13}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-50)-32-39}{6} \\\\  & =- \\frac{121}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543834 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04fa1ed4-4d14-540d-97de-17e4ea742baa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543807} {:answer/id #uuid "0ed577c8-30fd-548a-b930-79b44fab958d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ide